      8 +Why this step was picked:
      9 +- The timing report showed "PowerWorld contingency file" taking 2 min 28.0 sec.
     10 +- That was the largest remaining slow step after the model writer work.
     11 +- This step only changes the RawWriter contingency-file path. It does not change the model writer, form timing
         code, or Step 1 device-data updater.
     12 +
     13 +
     14 +Previous code notes:
     15 +
     16 +1. Line contingency loop
     17 +
     18 +Previous code did this:
     19 +
     20 +    Set Line = db.OpenRecordset("Line", dbOpenDynaset, dbSeeChanges)
     21 +    Line.MoveLast
     22 +    Line.MoveFirst
     23 +    While Not Line.EOF
     24 +        If Line.Fields("InclAsCTG") And Line.Fields("start_date") <= snapshotDate And snapshotDate < Line.Field
         s("end_date") Then
     25 +            QueueDependency = Not assessQueueDependency(Line.Fields("line_id"), incNoIA, ignoreAfterQueueNum)
     26 +        End If
     27 +        ...
     28 +        Line.MoveNext
     29 +    Wend
     30 +
     31 +What that means:
     32 +- It opened every Line row, even rows that were not included as contingencies.
     33 +- It opened inactive Line rows and then filtered them inside VBA.
     34 +- For every included line, it called assessQueueDependency using line_id.
     35 +- assessQueueDependency then queried the Line table again to get Queue and NotQueue, even though the current Li
         ne record already had those fields.
     36 +
     37 +
     38 +2. Branch lookup inside each line
     39 +
     40 +Previous code did this:
     41 +
     42 +    On Error Resume Next
     43 +    db.QueryDefs.Delete "filteredBranch"
     44 +    On Error GoTo WritePwConFile_Error
     45 +
     46 +    SQL = "SELECT * FROM Branch WHERE (((Branch.line_id)=" & Line.Fields("line_id") & "));"
     47 +    Set filteredBranchQuery = db.CreateQueryDef("filteredBranch", SQL)
     48 +    Set filteredBranch = db.OpenRecordset("filteredBranch", dbOpenDynaset, dbSeeChanges)
     49 +
     50 +What that means:
     51 +- For each line, Access deleted a saved query named filteredBranch.
     52 +- Then Access created a saved query named filteredBranch.
     53 +- Then Access opened that saved query.
     54 +- This repeats over and over during the contingency build.
     55 +- Creating/deleting saved QueryDefs is expensive and can add to Access database bloat.
     56 +- The query selected every Branch row for the line, then VBA checked branch start/end dates inside the loop.
     57 +
     58 +
     59 +3. Queue project lookup
     60 +
     61 +Previous code did this inside assessQueueDependency:
     62 +
     63 +    SQL = "SELECT QueuedProjects.Include, QueuedProjects.QueueNum, QueuedProjects.SignedIA, QueuedProjects.[W/D
         ] " & _
     64 +        "FROM QueuedProjects WHERE (((QueuedProjects.QueueNum)=" & lineQueueNum & "))"
     65 +    Set queuedProjectRecords = db.OpenRecordset(SQL, dbOpenDynaset, dbSeeChanges)
     66 +
     67 +What that means:
     68 +- If many lines use the same Queue or NotQueue number, Access still queried QueuedProjects again each time.
     69 +- The same queue decision could be recalculated many times in one build.
